From e9a75ad5bb4cc13a76a3f6550847780e49ae3e4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t5dr <t5dr@local>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 09:18:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] 89-VU1-and-GS-frontend-on-worker-threads-PS2X_VU1_ASYNC

Tekken 4 port (t4-perf). The EE thread ran the VU1 microprograms and every GS packet inside its own VIF1/GIF DMA
writes: about half of its work in a fight (with sound the game thread sat at the 16.7 ms limit on a fast PC).
Now the EE thread records that work in order -- VIF1 UNPACKs with the VIF registers they read
(ps2_vu1_async::applyVif1Unpack, the same code for both paths), MPG uploads, MSCAL/MSCNT with TOP/ITOP and MXCSR,
every GIF packet's bytes from the arbiter -- and a VU1 worker runs it; with 3+ logical processors a second worker
takes the GS frontend (PS2X_GS_SPLIT=0: one worker). The EE waits for empty queues before it can observe their
results: VBlankStart, GS privileged register reads/writes, EE loads/stores to VU1 memory, the GS stubs (clears,
local->host, presenter latch, display env, SetGsCrt), rollback capture/restore/hash and every change of the rollback
cuts. Frames with cuts, VU runs with D/T stop bits enabled, and the UNPACK form whose ROW update reads old VU1
lanes run synchronously as before. PS2X_VU1_ASYNC=0 = the old path.

Also: ps2_perf_stats::threadCycles() (processor cycles of the calling thread) and a [perf] vu1_async line
(records, KB, syncs, waits, fallbacks per second).

Checked: TEKKEN4_RB_MODE=measure with the full per-frame state hash (RDRAM, scratchpad, VU0/VU1 memory and
registers, scheduler, device and GS privileged registers): 6005 / 6071 / 6093 frames identical to the old path.
EE thread per fight frame 56-65 -> 27 Mcycles.
